course: dictionary
lesson: 10745
title: Direction words in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Upwind is a direction word. It tells you where the wind is coming from. Read the sentence around it to find the clues: «When acacias are eaten, their leaves become more bitter, and giraffes often move on and browse trees upwind.» The giraffes move to the side the wind comes from, so upwind means against the wind.
explain: Upwind is used as an adjective and an adverb. You can be upwind of something, as in «stand upwind of the bonfire», or you can sail upwind, which means sailing into the wind.
explain: Ask what would make sense in the sentence. If you stand upwind of a bonfire, the smoke blows away from you. Try your meaning in the sentence: the wind blows from you towards the fire, so the smoke goes the other way.
fact: Upwind means in the direction the wind is coming from.
fact: If you are upwind of something, the wind blows from you towards it.
fact: Standing upwind of a bonfire keeps the smoke blowing away from you.
fact: Hunters and animal watchers stay downwind, so animals cannot smell them.
fact: Sailors can sail upwind, but they have to zigzag, not go straight into the wind.
exercise: «Giraffes often browse trees upwind.» What does upwind mean? | *In the direction the wind is coming from | Under the shade of the tree | In the direction the wind is going to | High up in the sky
exercise: Sam is standing upwind of the bonfire. Which way does the wind blow? | Straight up into the sky | There is no wind at all | *From Sam towards the bonfire | From the bonfire towards Sam
exercise: Which is the best place to stand so the smoke blows away from you? | In the smoke, right above the flames | Downwind of the bonfire | *Upwind of the bonfire | Directly behind the bonfire, where the smoke is thickest
test:
Why do hunters stay downwind of animals? | *So the animals cannot smell them | So the animals can hear them more clearly | So the animals can see the hunters coming | So the hunters can get wet in the rain
Sailing upwind means what? | *Sailing into the wind, against it | Sailing with the wind pushing from behind | Sailing in a circle on still water | Sailing without any sails
exam:
In «giraffes often browse trees upwind», what does upwind tell us about where the giraffes are? | They are on the side the wind is coming from | *They are on the side the wind is coming from, so the wind blows from them towards the trees | They are underwater | They are in a different country
